.TH HEE-CRED 1 "2026-09-12" "HEE" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hee-cred \- real, minimal sealed credential store (GPG, or age for TPM-bound secrets)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hee cred
.B \-seal
.I ACCOUNT
.B \-recipients
.I ID[,ID...]
[\fB\-dir\fR \fIPATH\fR]
.br
.B hee cred
.B \-seal
.I ACCOUNT
.B \-recipients
.I ID[,ID...]
.B \-genfrom
.I TERM
[\fB\-blocks\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-root\fR \fIPATH\fR]
.br
.B hee cred
.B \-seal
.I ACCOUNT
.B \-backend age
.B \-recipients
.I RECIPIENT[,RECIPIENT...]
[\fB\-dir\fR \fIPATH\fR]
.br
.B hee cred
.B \-pass
.I ACCOUNT
.B \-exec
.I COMMAND [ARGS...]
[\fB\-dir\fR \fIPATH\fR] [\fB\-identity\fR \fIFILE\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hee-cred
stores secrets as ciphertext-only files under
.BR .hee/secrets/ACCOUNT.gpg ,
or
.B .hee/secrets/ACCOUNT.age
for the age backend, and never lets a decrypted value reach an agent's own
visible output.
Retrieval is exec-only: the secret is injected into a single child
process's environment and nowhere else.
.PP
.B \-seal
(typed) reads the secret from an interactive hidden prompt. It
refuses to run at all if stdin isn't a real terminal \-\- deliberately,
so a secret can never be piped in from a chat, log, or script.
.PP
.B \-seal \-genfrom
instead pulls real words out of a local media corpus matching
.IR TERM ,
joins them into a passphrase. Still never printed \-\- flows straight
into the same GPG encrypt call as a typed secret.
.PP
.B \-seal \-backend age
encrypts with
.BR age (1)
to age recipients instead of GPG keys. An age-plugin-tpm recipient
.RI ( age1tag1... )
binds the secret to one machine's TPM: nothing but that TPM can open it.
That is how a host being installed gets secrets it can use at first boot,
before it has any GPG key.
.PP
.B \-pass
decrypts and sets the secret in the
.B HEE_CRED_PASS
environment variable of the
.B \-exec
child process only \-\- never argv (visible via \fBps\fR to other
users), never stdout, never a log line. The backend is chosen by which
file exists; an account with both a
.B .gpg
and an
.B .age
file is refused rather than guessed at.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-seal " ACCOUNT"
Seal a new secret under the given account name.
.TP
.BI \-recipients " ID[,ID...]"
Comma\-separated GPG key IDs or emails to encrypt to, or age recipients
with
.BR "\-backend age" .
At least one required. Multiple recipients means any one of them can
independently decrypt.
.TP
.BI \-backend " gpg|age"
With
.BR \-seal :
the encryption backend (default
.BR gpg ).
.B age
writes
.I ACCOUNT.age
and needs
.B age
on PATH, plus
.B age\-plugin\-tpm
for a TPM recipient. Refused if the account already has a file from the
other backend.
.TP
.BI \-run " ACCOUNT"
Like
.B \-pass
, but also exports the secret under a variable name derived from
.IR ACCOUNT :
non-alphanumerics become underscores and the whole name is upper-cased, so
.I plex\-token
arrives as
.BR PLEX_TOKEN ,
.I cf\-dns\-token
as
.BR CF_DNS_TOKEN .
This is the naming operators were already applying by hand in every
.B \-exec
wrapper, so a consumer needs no
.B sh \-c
shim to rename it.
.B HEE_CRED_PASS
is still set as well, so existing wrappers keep working.
.TP
.BI \-as " VAR"
With
.B \-run
or
.BR \-pass :
use
.I VAR
as the variable name instead of the derived one. For a tool whose expected
variable does not follow from the credential's own name.
.TP
.BI \-pass " ACCOUNT"
Decrypt the named account's secret for one \fB\-exec\fR call.
.TP
.BI \-identity " FILE"
With
.B \-pass
or
.B \-run
on an
.B .age
credential: the age identity file to decrypt with. Defaults to
.BR HEE_CRED_AGE_IDENTITY .
A TPM identity file holds a key wrapped by that TPM and is useless on any
other machine.
.TP
.BI \-exec " COMMAND [ARGS...]"
The command to run with \fBHEE_CRED_PASS\fR set. Required with
\fB\-pass\fR.
.TP
.BI \-genfrom " TERM"
Generate a passphrase from real corpus text matching \fITERM\fR
instead of typing one.
.TP
.BI \-blocks " N"
Number of corpus\-derived words to join (default 4).
.TP
.BI \-dir " PATH"
Secrets directory (default \fB.hee/secrets\fR, relative to cwd).
.TP
.BI \-root " PATH"
Corpus root for \fB\-genfrom\fR (default \fB/var/local/hee\-corpus\fR).
.SH EXAMPLES
Seal a secret, two recipients:
.PP
.RS
hee cred \-seal my\-api\-key \e
.br
    \-recipients 6A58FE47...3188,73B1462A...FDA0
.RE
.PP
Use a sealed secret without ever seeing it:
.PP
.RS
hee cred \-pass my\-api\-key \-exec bash \-c \e
.br
    'curl \-H "Authorization: Bearer $HEE_CRED_PASS" https://api.example.com'
.RE
.PP
Seal a Wi\-Fi password to this machine's TPM, then open it on the same
machine (the identity comes from
.BR "age\-plugin\-tpm \-\-generate" ):
.PP
.RS
hee cred \-seal wifi \-backend age \e
.br
    \-recipients "$(age\-plugin\-tpm \-y identity.txt)" \-dir .hee/secrets
.br
hee cred \-run wifi \-identity identity.txt \-dir .hee/secrets \e
.br
    \-exec nmcli connection modify home wifi\-sec.psk\-flags 0 ...
.RE
.SH SECURITY
A secret sealed to multiple recipients can be decrypted independently
by any one of them \-\- this is intentional, not a weakness. Add every
real party that legitimately needs access as a recipient at seal
time; there is no way to add a recipient after the fact without
re\-sealing.
.PP
A TPM\-sealed secret dies with that TPM: a firmware TPM clear or a board
replacement makes it unrecoverable. Keep the plaintext's real source of
truth (a password manager) and treat the sealed copy as a delivery
vehicle, not a backup.
